          "title": "You'll want to know: 下午茶",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "下午茶 — xiàwǔchá — \"afternoon tea (a light meal)\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "我们三点吃下午茶 wǒmen sān diǎn chī xiàwǔchá — We have afternoon tea at three."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 茶 (chá) — nine strokes in three pieces: the grass piece of 菜 on top, the two strokes of 人 under it, and at the foot a horizontal, a vertical with a hook, and two short strokes on either side of it. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "下午茶 (xiàwǔchá) is built from 下午 'afternoon' and 茶 (chá) 'tea'. All three parts are inherited from Middle Chinese, haeX, nguX and drae (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription); the dictionary marks the tea character as a popular form. Tea with something to eat, as a light meal of its own."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
